│   └── UploadConnectionPool
│
├── latency/
│   ├── PingTester
│   └── JitterCalculator
│
└── metrics/
    ├── ThroughputCalculator
    ├── RollingAverage
    └── ResultProcessor
Phase 3 — Distributed Server Infrastructure
Objective

Replace the single Railway backend dependency.

Initial server regions

Deploy speed-test servers in:

India / Asia
Singapore
Europe
US East
US West
Server requirements

Every server must provide identical endpoints.

GET     /health
GET     /ping
GET     /download
POST    /upload
GET     /getIP
Server registry

Maintain a central registry.

Example structure:

Server Registry
│
├── Asia India
├── Asia Singapore
├── Europe
├── US East
└── US West

Each server must include:

ID.
Name.
Region.
Base URL.
Health status.
Backend type.
Priority.
Phase 4 — Server Discovery and Selection
Objective

Automatically select the best server for each user.

Process
Start Test
    ↓
Load Server Registry
    ↓
Check Server Health
    ↓
Ping Available Servers
    ↓
Measure Latency
    ↓
Remove Slow/Failed Servers
    ↓
Select Best Server
Server probing

For every available server:

Send multiple ping requests.
Measure round-trip latency.
Calculate average latency.
Calculate jitter.
Reject failed servers.
Rank healthy servers.
Selection rules

Primary selection:

Lowest latency

Secondary selection:

Server availability
+
Connection stability
+
Recent health status
Failover

If the selected server fails:

Selected Server Failed
        ↓
Cancel Current Request
        ↓
Select Next Best Server
        ↓
Restart Measurement
Phase 5 — Fast.com-Style Download Measurement
Objective

Replace fixed download requests with sustained adaptive measurement.

Download timeline
0–1 second
Warm-up
        ↓
1–3 seconds
Connection scaling
        ↓
3–10 seconds
Sustained measurement
        ↓
Final result
Connection scaling

Start with a small number of connections.

1
↓
2
↓
4
↓
8
↓
16
Adaptive rule

Increase connections only if additional connections improve throughput.

Example:

1 connection
↓
Measure throughput
↓
2 connections
↓
Measure improvement
↓
4 connections
↓
Measure improvement

Stop scaling when:

Throughput stabilizes
OR
Maximum connections reached
OR
Test duration reached
Phase 6 — Long-Running Download Streams
Current problem

Fixed-size downloads can produce inaccurate results.

New behavior

The backend must support sustained binary streaming.

Requirements
Binary response.
No compression.
No caching.
Long-running stream.
Efficient memory usage.
Proper backpressure handling.
Immediate cancellation support.
Request behavior

Each parallel connection continuously requests data during the test window.

The engine measures:

Total Bytes Received
÷
Measurement Time

But only after warm-up and stabilization logic.

Phase 7 — Throughput Sampling System
Objective

Avoid calculating speed from one large request.

Sampling

Collect measurements periodically.

Example:

Sample 1
Sample 2
Sample 3
Sample 4
Sample 5
...

Each sample records:

Timestamp
Bytes Downloaded
Instant Throughput
Active Connections
Processing

The engine must:

Ignore warm-up samples.
Detect abnormal spikes.
Detect stalled connections.
Calculate rolling averages.
Detect stable throughput.
Produce final sustained speed.
Final result

Use sustained performance instead of peak bursts.

Peak Speed      → informational only
Sustained Speed → primary result
Phase 8 — Accurate Download Result Calculation
Measurement rules

Do not use:

Maximum observed speed

as the final result.

Instead:

Warm-up exclusion
+
Stable measurement window
+
Rolling average
+
Outlier handling
=
Final Download Speed
Metrics

Calculate:

Current speed.
Average speed.
Sustained speed.
Peak speed.
Total bytes transferred.
Test duration.
Phase 9 — Upload Test Engine
Objective

Add Fast.com-style upload measurement.

Process
Generate Binary Data
        ↓
Start Upload Connections
        ↓
Scale Connections
        ↓
Measure Sustained Upload
        ↓
Calculate Final Result
Connection scaling

Use the same adaptive pattern:

1 → 2 → 4 → 8

The upload backend must:

Accept streaming requests.
Immediately consume data.
Never store test data.
Support cancellation.
Support concurrent connections.
Result calculation

Use:

Sustained uploaded bytes
÷
Measurement window
Phase 10 — Latency and Jitter Measurement
Ping testing

Perform multiple requests:

Ping 1
Ping 2
Ping 3
Ping 4
Ping 5

Calculate:

Average Latency
Jitter

Calculate variation between latency measurements.

Example:

Ping Results

20ms
22ms
19ms
24ms
21ms

Result:

Latency → Average
Jitter  → Variation
Phase 11 — Test State Machine

Create a strict test lifecycle.

IDLE
 ↓
INITIALIZING
 ↓
DISCOVERING_SERVERS
 ↓
PROBING_SERVERS
 ↓
SELECTING_SERVER
 ↓
TESTING_LATENCY
 ↓
TESTING_DOWNLOAD
 ↓
TESTING_UPLOAD
 ↓
PROCESSING_RESULTS
 ↓
COMPLETED
Error states
FAILED
CANCELLED
SERVER_UNREACHABLE
NETWORK_ERROR
TIMEOUT
Important rule

The UI must never control measurement logic directly.

The engine controls the test state.

The UI only displays:

Engine State
+
Metrics
Phase 12 — Real-Time UI Integration
Preserve existing design

Keep:

Current speedometer.
Existing animations.
Dark/glassmorphic UI.
Result screens.
Existing controls.
Connect UI to real metrics

The gauge must use:

Live Throughput Samples

not simulated values.

UI updates

During download:

Current Speed
Average Speed
Server Region
Latency
Active Connections

During upload:

Current Upload
Average Upload
Active Connections
Phase 13 — Backend Improvements

Every speed-test server must support the same API.

Health endpoint
GET /health

Used for:

Server availability.
Monitoring.
Failover.
Ping endpoint
GET /ping

Requirements:

Small response.
No cache.
Minimal processing.
Download endpoint
GET /download

Requirements:

Binary stream
No cache
No compression
Sustained response
Concurrent connections
Upload endpoint
POST /upload

Requirements:

Streaming body
Discard data
No database storage
Concurrent requests
Phase 14 — Infrastructure Requirements
Do not depend on one server

Current:

User
 ↓
Railway
 ↓
Result

Target:

                     ┌── India
                     │
                     ├── Singapore
User ────────────────┼── Europe
                     │
                     ├── US East
                     │
                     └── US West
Infrastructure requirements

Each test server should provide:

High network bandwidth.
Public HTTPS access.
Stable network routing.
Sufficient concurrent connections.
Geographic diversity.
Important

A low-capacity backend will become the bottleneck.

The test result should measure:

User Internet Capacity

not:

Server Bandwidth Limit
Phase 15 — Configuration System

Use a server registry environment configuration.

Example concept:

NEXT_PUBLIC_SPEEDTEST_SERVERS

The registry should support multiple servers.

Each entry contains:

id
name
region
baseUrl
enabled
priority
Development configuration

Allow:

localhost

only in development.

Production

Only use:

HTTPS public servers
Phase 16 — Error Handling and Failover

Handle:

Server failure
Server returns 5xx
↓
Mark unhealthy
↓
Select next server
Timeout
Request timeout
↓
Abort connection
↓
Retry or failover
Network loss
Connection lost
↓
Cancel test
↓
Show network error
Partial failure

If one parallel connection fails:

Remove failed connection
↓
Continue remaining connections
↓
Replace connection if required
Phase 17 — Anti-Inflation Protection

The engine should prevent artificially high results.

Avoid
Short burst measurement
Avoid
Single request result
Avoid
Peak speed as final speed
Use
Warm-up
+
Sustained streaming
+
Rolling samples
+
Stable measurement window
Phase 18 — Testing Strategy
Local testing

Test:

Low-speed network.
High-speed network.
Network interruption.
Server failure.
Slow server.
Multiple browser tabs.
Test cancellation.
Production testing

Compare results against:

Fast.com.
Speedtest.net.
Cloudflare Speed Test.
Expected rule

Results do not need to be identical.

But results should be:

Consistent
+
Repeatable
+
Reasonably close

Large unexplained differences must be investigated.

Phase 19 — Performance and Resource Safety
Backend

Ensure:

No test data storage.
No memory leaks.
Request cancellation handling.
Connection limits.
Backpressure support.
Frontend

Ensure:

AbortController for all test requests.
Cleanup on page navigation.
Cleanup on cancellation.
No orphaned streams.
Controlled connection scaling.
Phase 20 — Security and Abuse Protection

Speed-test endpoints can be abused because they intentionally consume bandwidth.

Implement:

Rate limiting.
Request limits.
Concurrent test limits.
Abuse detection.
DDoS protection where supported.
Server-side time limits.
Maximum connection limits.

Do not require login for normal speed tests.

